WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.910
This is the gala guest list tool my team of four built for a New York nonprofit.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.350
I led the backend.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.030
Here's one gala, start to finish, on invented data.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.590
Chapter one: setting up the gala.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:20.440
Mei, the gala admin, signs in with a six-digit code from her email.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.760
There are no passwords, and only people on the staff list get in.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.630
Gala information holds the date, the venue, and the deadlines.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.830
Emails and the sponsor portal read them from here.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.200
Each sponsorship level has its price, and how many guests come with it.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.730
Give Lively, the ticketing site, sold more overnight.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.170
Mei uploads last night's export,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.690
then links each of its pages and ticket types to a sponsorship package, once.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.750
Then she imports.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.010
Twenty-seven new orders come in, and twenty-one guest names are filled in for her.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.850
The close calls, like a possible duplicate guest or a refund,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.470
wait for a person to decide.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.430
Chapter two: a sponsor pays, and gets their link.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.580
Daniel is a relationship manager.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.620
Acme still owes twenty-five thousand dollars, and five of its seats have no names.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.570
Acme's check came in the mail.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.190
Daniel records it: twelve thousand five hundred dollars,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.550
the check number, and the day it arrived.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.670
The app never takes card or bank details.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.830
A guest list reminder was drafted on its own.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.470
It goes out only when staff approve it.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.350
Chapter three: the sponsor names their guests.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.000
Jordan, at Acme, gets the reminder.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.740
The button opens Acme's private sponsor portal, with no account needed.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.810
Five of ten guests are named so far, and the check already shows as partially paid.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.140
Jordan adds Avery Kim.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.320
Each guest gets a name, a title for the badge,

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.480
an email, and any food or access needs.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.300
Avery is vegetarian, with no access needs.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:11.220
Then Riley Chen, Director of Partnerships, with no dietary restrictions.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.960
Seven of ten seats are named now.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.800
The last three are due by April twenty-third,

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.540
and Jordan can edit any of them until then.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.930
Chapter four: staff get everyone ready.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.320
Back in the staff app, Daniel reviews every name a sponsor adds,

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.300
starting with Avery and Riley.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.910
The guest list works like a spreadsheet, and every change is timestamped.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.810
A search for Acme finds its seven guests.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.130
Daniel seats Avery and Riley together at table eight.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.870
Then he goes back to Acme's order and marks both badges ready.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.230
The Executive Director has invited Sam Ortega, from a local arts collective.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.530
Daniel adds Sam as a relationship guest: a complimentary seat,

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.030
with no payment and no sponsor.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.150
A note says who invited them.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.640
Daniel previews Sam's invitation: the date, the venue, and when the doors open.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.020
A new personal QR code is made only when it sends,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.520
and the database keeps only its hash.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:19.060
The code is for Sam alone, and it works until the morning after the gala.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.770
Mei exports every badge as one file for the printer.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.580
Chapter five: gala night, at the door.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:30.580
Gala night.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:34.800
Kenji, a volunteer, signs in on the iPad with his own code.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.880
He sees the door list and nothing else: five hundred seventy-seven guests,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.040
synced live.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.010
He searches for Avery, taps check in, and Avery's in.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.210
Undo stays up for ten seconds.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.760
Sam holds up the ticket.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.900
The camera reads it, and the iPad says who came in.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.720
Jamie Walker isn't on the list, but Mei says yes.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.860
Kenji adds a walk-in as an exception,

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.900
with the sponsor and a note on who approved it.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.400
It stays pending until staff confirm it.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.780
At the top, the iPad shows it's synced, live.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.300
Then the Wi-Fi drops.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:17.040
Kenji searches for Riley Chen and taps check in anyway, with no connection at all.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.640
The check-in waits on the iPad, and when the Wi-Fi is back, it sends itself.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.940
If two iPads check in the same guest, the first arrival wins.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.760
Chapter six: after the doors close.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.150
Back on the dashboard, arrivals count up live from the door.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.970
And for the thank-you notes: who came, one row per guest.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.130
Only gala admins can download it, because it holds emails.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:49.160
My part was the backend: who can see and change what,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.180
the private links and tickets, and the sync behind the door.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.800
Every person and record here is invented.
